description:
crate-jmap-sharing-server/src/memory.rs:353-371. The change-log dedup loop:

    for entry in &relevant {
        for id in &entry.created {
            if !destroyed.contains(id) && !created.contains(id) {
                created.push(id.clone());
            }
        }
        ...
        for id in &entry.destroyed {
            created.retain(|c| c != id);
            updated.retain(|u| u != id);
            if !destroyed.contains(id) {
                destroyed.push(id.clone());
            }
        }
    }

Trigger: a sequence of change-log entries on the same account where:
  entry K:   created = [id1]
  entry K+1: destroyed = [id1]
  entry K+2: created = [id1]   (id recycled — see bd:JMAP-qz9v.14 demo_next_id collision)

After entry K+1 the local 'destroyed' contains id1. Entry K+2's create loop sees 'destroyed.contains(id1) == true' and SKIPS adding id1 to created. The final response has destroyed=[id1] but created=[]. The current state has id1 actually present (last entry created it). The client receiving this response thinks id1 was destroyed and never restored, but its next /get of id1 returns the object. State drift.

The RFC 8620 §5.2 semantics: 'destroyed' contains records destroyed since sinceState, 'created' contains records created since sinceState. An id that was created → destroyed → created in the interval is a created record relative to sinceState (the final state has it). The current loop produces incoherent output.

Two fixes:
  (a) Change the order: an entry's create should OVERRIDE a prior destroyed:
        for id in &entry.created {
            destroyed.retain(|d| d != id);
            if !created.contains(id) { created.push(id.clone()); }
        }
  (b) Stop the upstream collision (bd:JMAP-qz9v.14) — once ids are not recycled, this code path is unreachable. But the underlying invariant in this loop is still wrong; defense in depth says fix both.

Sibling-scope: the same loop appears in 6 sibling MemoryBackends (chat, calendars, contacts, metadata, sharing, tasks). Mail-server is uuid-based so doesn't recycle; the loop is still present and same shape but is unreachable in practice. Propagation belongs in the same sweep as bd:JMAP-qz9v.14.
